  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Wednesday)...\n\nAs a surface high shifts east, return easterly flow will tap into\nplains moisture and advect it into the area in the early morning \nhours, allowing for isolated showers (10-30% chance) over far \nsoutheast Montana.\n\nOver the west, isolated showers and thunderstorms (10-30% chance)\nare expected this morning ahead of a progressive trough moving \ninto the region by this afternoon. As the trough moves east and \napproaches the area of better moisture, scattered thunderstorms \n(40-60% chance) will spread across the region this afternoon and \nevening. Strong to severe storms are possible from Billings east, \nwith the main threats being hail up to golf ball size and wind \ngusts of 60+ mph. There is also potential for locally heavy \nrainfall with any storm and an isolated tornado threat, with the \nhighest probabilities from Broadus east across southern Carter \nCounty. Keep weather aware and have a plan to seek shelter if \nstorms approach your location. \n\nUpper level ridging builds in for Friday, bringing warmer and \nmostly dry conditions. A deepening Pacific trough over the west \ncoast will gradually turn flow out of the southwest, allowing wind\ngusts to increase to 20 to 40 mph over the mountains and \nfoothills (highest over the mountains) Friday afternoon.\n\nEarly Saturday looks to remain warm and dry ahead of the \napproaching trough. As upper level flow continues to turn more \nsouthwesterly, some energy will enter the region. Coupled with a \nlate day cold front, this will be sufficient to generate scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms (30-50% chance) over eastern Montana \nand into the Dakotas. There is still uncertainty in the exact \ntiming of the cold front, which will influence where storms \ndevelop.\n\nBehind the cold front Sunday will be cooler and unsettled, with \nwidespread scattered showers and thunderstorms (40-60% chance) \nthrough the day. Into at least the middle of next week, conditions\nlook to remain unsettled under the influence of a Pacific trough.\n\nHighs today will be in the 70s to low 80s, increasing to \ngenerally 80s to low 90s by Saturday. Sunday will be cooler, with \nhighs ranging from 60s over the west to near 80 over the east. \nGoing into next week, highs will warm back towards the 80s. Archer",
